  "query_preview": "..Low pressure moving through the northern Rockies today \nwill continue to push a drier air mass eastward into our region. \nThis is reflected in the satellite precipitable water estimates \nshowing PW values falling to <0.50\" over the high country \ncompared to 0.80\"+ yesterday at this time. Enough moisture lingers\nover the far eastern portions of the forecast area (Apache \nCounty) for some isolated to scattered thunderstorm activity later\nthis afternoon, and some of this could produce additional \nlocalized heavy rain. Also, as the trough axis swings by to our \nnorth later this evening, a few showers or storms could develop \nnorth of I-40 to the AZ/UT line, and a 10-30% PoP is in the \nforecast in that area. By Saturday and Sunday, models show \ncontinued drying as westerly flow persists. Precipitable water \nvalues fall even more, to under 0.30\" for most locations with \ndewpoints in the 20s and 30s. Pleasant daytime temperatures close \nto normal will be followed by some cool nights. With these lower \ndewpoints, expect some high elevation locations to fall into the \n30s overnight, with near-freezing temperatures Sat-Mon mornings at\nthe typical cold spots (Bellemont, Tusayan, Ft. Valley). \n\nMonday through Tuesday - a low pressure system is forecast to cross \nwest to east across the region. Much of the guidance has it tracking \nthrough southern NV into UT Monday night into Tuesday. Most of \nMonday is looking dry at this time, with rain chances entering the \nforecast Monday evening in NW Arizona, spreading eastward through \nthe night and into Tuesday. Moisture return ahead of the trough \nlooks sufficient for some showers and thunderstorms to develop, but \nforecast PW values are nothing like we saw earlier this week so the \nthreat of heavy rainfall/flooding is fairly low. The exception may \nbe right along the AZ/NM state line as deeper moisture in New Mexico \ncould be drawn in to that area so that is something to watch. \nWednesday through Friday - majority of ensemble members indicate \nhigh pressure strengthening over the south central United States, \nwith south-southwest flow aloft over our area. Moisture values are \nforecast to remain sufficient for daily thunderstorm chances, \ncurrently in the 20-50% range each day. Daytime temperatures \nforecast to be within a few degrees of normal.",
